# politicas — environment variables (template)
#
# This file documents variable NAMES only. It contains NO secrets and NO real
# values. Copy to `.env.local` for local dev (git-ignored), and set the real
# values in the Vercel dashboard for Production and Preview environments
# (Project → Settings → Environment Variables). See docs/deploy/vercel.md.
#
# Every var below is OPTIONAL for the app to RUN: with none set, the public
# surfaces work normally and Sentry is a completely silent no-op (no init, no
# network, no console noise). Two of them gate capability, not startup:
# without ADMIN_TOKEN the operator console stays locked, without REVIEWER_TOKEN
# the money write path stays read-only. Both fail CLOSED — unset is never open.
#
# The NAME of every variable is constant per environment; the VALUE is set per
# deployment target: `.env.local` locally (git-ignored), CI secrets in the
# workflow, and the host dashboard (Vercel → Project → Settings → Environment
# Variables) for Preview/Production. Never commit a value here.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sentry — error & performance monitoring (client + server)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN — the single gate for ALL Sentry activity.
#   Unset  → Sentry.init is never called anywhere (silent no-op).
#   Set    → client, server, and edge runtimes report to this DSN, and that
#            includes the React render-error boundaries app/global-error.tsx +
#            app/error.tsx (both call Sentry.captureException; with no DSN the
#            call is a no-op and the fallback UI still renders).
#   Public by design (embedded in the client bundle); not a secret.
#   NAME constant per environment, VALUE per deployment target:
#     Local dev  → .env.local
#     CI         → repository/workflow secret
#     Preview / Production → the host dashboard (Vercel env vars)
#   >>> Currently commented out here and unset everywhere, which means ZERO
#   >>> environments report today: crashes are caught and shown honestly, but
#   >>> nobody is told about them. Set the DSN per environment to change that.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=

# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ENV — environment tag attached to every Sentry event. Same
#   contract: the NAME is constant, the VALUE identifies the deployment target
#   (this is what separates prod noise from a preview experiment in Sentry).
#     Production → production
#     Preview    → preview
#     Local dev  → development
# NEXT_PUBLIC_APP_ENV=development

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Sentry — build-time source-map upload (optional; CI / Vercel build only)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Only needed to un-minify stack traces in Sentry. If any are unset, the build
# skips source-map upload silently — it never fails the build. Do NOT commit a
# real SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN; set it only in Vercel (marked as a secret) and/or CI.

# SENTRY_ORG=
# SENTRY_PROJECT=
# SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reviewer write path — /penize/kontrola human-verification console (Case ①)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Gates the ONLY write path on the platform: confirming/rejecting a pending
# MP↔company tie. Single-operator console — one shared token, not per-user auth.
#   Unset REVIEWER_TOKEN → the write path is OFF; the console stays read-only
#   and states this honestly ("not-configured"), it never fails silently.
#   Set both → the console accepts decisions whose submitted token matches
#   REVIEWER_TOKEN exactly; REVIEWER_NAME is stamped as the reviewer on every
#   review_audit row and kg_edge.props update.
#   BOTH ARE REQUIRED TOGETHER (2026-08-04). With REVIEWER_TOKEN set but
#   REVIEWER_NAME unset the server REFUSES every decision ("misconfigured")
#   and the console says so before anything is written: decisions enter a
#   tamper-evident hash chain, and a chain that cannot say WHO decided is a
#   log, not an audit. It used to fall back to the literal name "reviewer".
# Server-only secrets — do NOT prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_.

# REVIEWER_NAME=
# REVIEWER_TOKEN=

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Operator console — /admin (case-loop run status, review queue, system state)
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# /admin exposes internal pipeline state. `robots: { index: false }` asks
# crawlers to look away; it is NOT access control. ADMIN_TOKEN is.
#   Unset ADMIN_TOKEN → the console is CLOSED and says so honestly
#   ("Nenastaveno" — an unconfigured lock is not an open door); getAdminData()
#   is never even called, so no internal state is loaded, let alone rendered.
#   Set → /admin asks for the token once, stores it in an httpOnly cookie
#   scoped to /admin for 12 h, and verifies it in constant time on every
#   request (shared gate: lib/security/token.ts, same code as REVIEWER_TOKEN).
# One shared operator token, not per-user auth. Server-only secret — do NOT
# prefix with NEXT_PUBLIC_, and use a different value than REVIEWER_TOKEN.

# ADMIN_TOKEN=
